<!--
  HueTheme.xaml · shared dark theme for the icon-family app suite
  =================================================================
  One file, pure XAML, no code and no packages. Works in any WPF app on .NET 6 or later.
  The accent hue encodes the app's domain, exactly like the icon set: every app in the
  suite looks the same except for its family colour.

  ADOPTING IT
    1. Copy this file into the project, e.g. Theme/HueTheme.xaml (Build Action: Page,
       the default for .xaml files).
    2. Merge it in App.xaml:
         <Application.Resources>
           <ResourceDictionary>
             <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
               <ResourceDictionary Source="Theme/HueTheme.xaml" />
             </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
           </ResourceDictionary>
         </Application.Resources>
    3. On every Window set Style="{StaticResource Hue.Window}". Implicit Window styles do
       not reach windows that derive from Window, which is every window you write.
    4. Add the companion HueThemeKit.cs (namespace HueThemeKit): the two things XAML cannot
       do. In App.OnStartup call HueContrast.Follow(this), which hands the colours back to
       Windows while a contrast theme is on. In every window's constructor, after
       InitializeComponent, call HueTitleBar.Attach(this) for the dark title bar.
    5. The full guide, including how to move an existing app onto this theme, is
       HUETHEME-HANDOVER.md. An AI assistant should read that before writing any code.

  PICKING THE FAMILY
    The ACTIVE block below says which family this copy is configured for. To change it,
    replace the family prefix in that block ("Cyan." becomes e.g. "Red."). Surfaces use
    "Neutral." for neutral surfaces, or "<Family>.Tint." (e.g. "Red.Tint.") for surfaces
    faintly tinted towards the family hue. Update the CONFIGURED FOR line to match.

      Family    Base     Mid      Light    Pale     Fill     FillHover OnAccent  Domain
      Cyan      #22D3EE  #38BDF8  #7DD3FC  #A4E0FD  #22D3EE  #38BDF8   #0B0D11   dev and tools
      Teal      #2DD4BF  #5EEAD4  #99F6E4  #B8F9EC  #2DD4BF  #5EEAD4   #0B0D11   system and file utilities
      Green     #22C55E  #4ADE80  #86EFAC  #AAF4C5  #22C55E  #4ADE80   #0B0D11   web and content creation
      Indigo    #5B5EEE  #818CF8  #A5B4FC  #C0CAFD  #5B5EEE  #4C4FE0   #FFFFFF   media
      Amber     #F59E0B  #FBBF24  #FCD34D  #FDE082  #F59E0B  #FBBF24   #0B0D11   data and registry
      Rose      #F43F5E  #FB7185  #FDA4AF  #FEBFC7  #E11D48  #C8173F   #FFFFFF   heritage and personal
      Red       #EF4444  #F87171  #FCA5A5  #FDC0C0  #DC2626  #C42222   #FFFFFF   comms and games
      Platinum  #94A3B8  #CBD5E1  #E2E8F0  #EBEFF4  #94A3B8  #CBD5E1   #0B0D11   oversight and authority

    Base is the accent itself: markers, lines, selection bars, focus borders. Fill and
    FillHover are the primary button and checked-box fill; OnAccent is the text on them.
    They equal Base and Mid except where the bright hue cannot carry readable text
    (Indigo, Rose, Red): there Fill is a deeper shade under white text, and hover deepens.
    Light is accent-coloured text and every glow. Pale is only used by the busy rings.
    Soft (14%), SoftHover (24%) and Edge (40%) are Base at reduced alpha, for tonal fills
    and their borders.

  RULES FOR VIEWS
    * Reference brushes with DynamicResource, never StaticResource, so family and tint
      can change at runtime. Styles, metrics, fonts and icons can be StaticResource.
    * Never write a hex value in a view. If a colour is missing, add a token here.
    * Hue marks meaning; glow marks importance. Built-in glow: the primary and danger
      buttons, critical callouts, the selected tab, the active nav item, a switched-on knob,
      a signal switch knob when safe or unsafe,
      the slider thumb and the header icon. Opt-in glow (Tag="Glow"): a single standalone
      pill, HTTP chip or progress bar. Never glow on anything that repeats in list or grid
      rows: every glow is a blur, and over Remote Desktop WPF draws blurs on the CPU.
    * Status colours (Ok, Warn, Limit, Error, Stale) are fixed. They do not follow the
      family, so a red "Failed" pill means the same thing in every app.
    * Switches never use the family colour. A preference switch is green when on and
      neutral otherwise. A signal switch shows what its state means: green safe, red
      unsafe, amber never configured, the same in every app, in the same soft glowing look.
    * Status ("how it went") is a pill; category ("which kind": Allow/Block, In/Out, a
      source) is a tag. A category without risk is a neutral tag; at most one category per
      view takes the accent (Hue.Tag.Accent). A category that carries risk uses the signal
      tags: green safe, amber caution, red unsafe, decided from the user's point of view.
    * Text: Brush.Text primary, Brush.TextDim secondary, Brush.TextMuted captions and
      section labels, Brush.TextDisabled.
    * Surfaces, darkest to lightest: Base (window), Chrome (menu, header, nav, status
      bar), Surface (cards), Raised (buttons, popups), Hover. Inputs sit on Base so they
      read as inset.

  ACCESSIBILITY (built to WCAG 2.2 AA)
    * Text is at least 4.5:1 everywhere: TextMuted is the dimmest text allowed.
    * Anything that identifies a control or its state is at least 3:1: control outlines use
      Brush.ControlEdge, checked and selected states use the accent, selected items in
      lists, grids and trees carry an accent bar, not just a tint.
    * Button text is at least 4.5:1 at rest and on hover in every family: primary buttons
      use Brush.AccentFill and Brush.OnAccent, destructive ones Brush.ErrorSolid and
      Brush.OnError. Never put text on Brush.Accent or Brush.Error directly.
    * Keyboard focus is always visible: an outset ring on controls, an inset ring on items
      in lists, grids and drop-downs, the accent border on inputs.
    * Targets are at least 24 x 24 (buttons 34, scroll bars 14 wide, tree expanders 24).
    * Status is never colour alone: pills carry a word. In the Red and Rose families the
      accent is close to the error red, so there a bare dot must never mean a status.
    * A Windows contrast theme wins: HueContrast maps every brush to system colours.
    * Card borders and dividers (LineSoft, Line) are decoration and may stay soft; never use
      them as the only outline of something the user operates.

  BUSY OVERLAY (BusyOverlayKit)
    <busy:BusyOverlay AnimateOverride="True"
                      ScrimBrush="{DynamicResource Busy.Scrim}"
                      RingBrush="{DynamicResource Busy.Ring}"
                      RingAccentBrush="{DynamicResource Busy.RingAccent}"
                      GlowBrush="{DynamicResource Brush.Glow}"
                      TitleBrush="{DynamicResource Brush.Text}"
                      DetailBrush="{DynamicResource Brush.TextDim}"
                      BarBackground="{DynamicResource Brush.Raised}"
                      BarForeground="{DynamicResource Brush.Accent}"
                      CancelStyle="{DynamicResource Hue.Button}"
                      DetailFontFamily="{DynamicResource Font.Mono}" />

  STYLE INDEX
    Window      Hue.Window
    Text        Text.Title  Text.Heading  Text.TileTitle  Text.Body  Text.Caption  Text.Section
                Text.Mono
    Buttons     Hue.Button (default)  Hue.Button.Primary  Hue.Button.Tonal
                Hue.Button.Outline  Hue.Button.Ghost  Hue.Button.Danger  Hue.Button.Icon
                Hue.Button.Indigo (app extra, fixed Indigo fill)
    Toggles     CheckBox and RadioButton (default)  Hue.Switch (CheckBox/ToggleButton)
                Hue.Switch.SafeWhenOn  Hue.Switch.SafeWhenOff   (signal: safe/unsafe/unset)
                HueSwitch (HueThemeKit.cs): three states, click-to-position, confirmable
    Layout      Hue.Card  Hue.Header  Hue.StatusBar  Hue.Divider
    Focus       Hue.FocusVisual (controls)  Hue.FocusVisual.Inset (items in lists and grids)
    Navigation  Hue.NavItem (RadioButton)  Hue.Swatch (RadioButton, Background = colour)
    Feedback    Hue.Callout  Hue.Callout.Critical
                Hue.Pill  Hue.Pill.Accent  Hue.Pill.Ok  Hue.Pill.Warn  Hue.Pill.Limit
                Hue.Pill.Error  Hue.Pill.Stale
                Hue.Tag  Hue.Tag.Accent                                  (category words)
                Hue.Tag.Safe  Hue.Tag.Caution  Hue.Tag.Unsafe           (categories with risk)
                Hue.Code.Ok  Hue.Code.Info  Hue.Code.Limit  Hue.Code.Error   (HTTP status chips)
                Hue.Stamp.Current  Hue.Stamp.Aged  Hue.Stamp.Old        (timestamp chips)
                Hue.Marker  Hue.Marker.Glow   (dots; colour = Foreground)
    Media       Hue.IconGlow (ContentControl around the app_256.png header image)
    Icons       Hue.Icon (Path style) with Icon.* geometries, 16 x 16, stroke = Foreground
    Implicit    Button ToggleButton RepeatButton TextBox PasswordBox CheckBox RadioButton
                ComboBox Slider ProgressBar ScrollBar ScrollViewer ListBox TreeView
                TabControl DataGrid Menu ContextMenu ToolTip Expander GroupBox Label
-->
<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=mscorlib">

